// src/v4/std/lexing.dag
// Scope: canonical lexical analysis substrate types.
// Anchor: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lexical_analysis
// Ref: Kleene (1956) — regular expression algebra (LexPattern structure).
// Ref: Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman "Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools"
//      (Dragon Book, 2nd ed.) §3.1 (tokens, patterns, lexemes), §3.3.3 (maximal munch).
// Status: substrate home for lexical analysis domain types; v4.compiler.tokenize
//         currently re-declares LexPattern/LexRule/LexRuleSet/Token as incumbent forms
//         pending migration to import from here.
//
// This file is the single substrate authority for the five concepts named in
// Wikipedia "Lexical analysis":
//   Token      — the unit emitted by the tokenizer (class + lexeme + source span).
//   Lexeme     — the matched character sequence from source (coincides with String;
//                see comment below — not declared as a separate type).
//   LexCharClass — character classification discriminant used inside LexPattern.
//   LexPattern — describes the form lexemes of a token class can take (Kleene algebra).
//   LexRule    — a (pattern, token-class) pair, or a trivia pattern to discard.
//   LexRuleSet — an ordered collection of LexRules under maximal-munch policy.
//
// Consumers (pending migration):
//   src/v4/compiler/01_tokenize.dag   — tokenizer walk + LexPattern match engine
//   src/v4/extdeps/languages/*.dag    — per-language lex rule declarations


module v4.std.lexing


import v4.std.collection { List }
import v4.std.node { Symbol }
import v4.std.text { Char, String }


// -- Lexeme (concept, not a separate type) -----------------------------------
// Wikipedia "Lexical analysis" § Lexeme:
//   "A lexeme is a sequence of characters in the source program that matches
//    the pattern for a token and is identified by the lexical analyzer as an
//    instance of that token."
// Structural coincidence with String (= FreeMonoid<Char>): a lexeme IS a
// sequence of characters — no additional structural facts distinguish the two.
// Grounding evidence: Wikipedia definition maps to FreeMonoid<Char> exactly;
// no spec facts are dropped by using String as the carrier.
// Role surfaces as the `lexeme: String` field on Token below.
// Not declared as a type alias to avoid a hollow alias (P1: hollow-alias problem shape).


// -- LexCharClass ------------------------------------------------------------
// Character classification for lexical analysis — discriminant for CharClassPattern
// in LexPattern. Variants cover the named character sets used by standard programming-
// language lex rules.
// Grounded in: Dragon Book §3.5 (character-class notation in lex specifications);
// standard ASCII-oriented lex character classes.
// Note: v4.compiler.tokenize declares LexCharClass with a Char-suffixed naming
// convention (WhitespaceChar, DigitChar, …) as the incumbent form; this is the
// canonical-name authority. Migration: v4.compiler.tokenize → imports LexCharClass
// from v4.std.lexing (follow-up, tracked via 🟡 CP-1b item 13a).
// 🟡 coproduct dissolution — CP-1b item 13a; lexical data is modeled, per-arm
//    lexer morphism witness remains gated.
type LexCharClass
  = Whitespace
  | Digit
  | HexDigit
  | IdentStart
  | IdentContinue
  | StringText
  | LineCommentText
  | BlockCommentText


// -- LexPattern --------------------------------------------------------------
// A lex pattern describes the form that lexemes of a token class can take.
// Ref: Wikipedia "Lexical analysis" § Pattern.
// Algebraic structure grounds in Kleene (1956) regular expression algebra:
//   EmptyPattern          — ε (empty string, always matches consuming nothing)
//   LiteralPattern        — exact literal text match
//   KeywordPattern        — literal text with identifier-boundary guard (keyword vs ident)
//   CharPattern           — exact single character match
//   CharClassPattern      — character class membership
//   SequencePattern       — concatenation (rs): left then right
//   ChoicePattern         — alternation (r|s): left if it matches, else right
//   OptionalPattern       — optionality (r?): match r or accept empty
//   RepeatPattern         — Kleene star (r*): zero or more, greedy, zero-width guarded
//   OneOrMorePattern      — Kleene plus (r+): one or more, greedy, zero-width guarded
//   DelimitedPattern      — open-body-close with try-close-first semantics; used for
//                           block constructs (e.g. block comments /**/) where the close
//                           delimiter may share characters with the body element.
// 🟡 coproduct dissolution — CP-1b item 13b; declarative parse/emit pattern data
//    is modeled, bidirectional grammar carrier convergence remains gated.
type LexPattern
  = EmptyPattern
  | LiteralPattern { text: String }
  | KeywordPattern { text: String }
  | CharPattern { char: Char }
  | CharClassPattern { class: LexCharClass }
  | SequencePattern { left: LexPattern, right: LexPattern }
  | ChoicePattern { left: LexPattern, right: LexPattern }
  | OptionalPattern { element: LexPattern }
  | RepeatPattern { element: LexPattern }
  | OneOrMorePattern { element: LexPattern }
  | DelimitedPattern { open: LexPattern, body: LexPattern, close: LexPattern }


// -- LexRule -----------------------------------------------------------------
// A lex rule pairs a lex pattern with the token class it produces, or marks a
// pattern as trivia (whitespace, comments) to be discarded without emitting a token.
// Ref: Wikipedia "Lexical analysis" § Tokenization; Dragon Book §3.1.
// 🟡 coproduct dissolution — CP-1b item 13c; token/trivia rule data is modeled,
//    per-arm lexer morphism witness remains gated.
type LexRule
  = TokenRule { token_class: Symbol, pattern: LexPattern }
  | TriviaRule { pattern: LexPattern }


// -- LexRuleSet --------------------------------------------------------------
// An ordered collection of lex rules.
// Rule order determines tie-breaking under maximal-munch (longest-match) policy:
// equal-length accepting matches prefer the later rule, so specific keyword or
// literal rules placed after the general identifier rule win (e.g., keyword "if"
// listed after ident pattern claims the lexeme over a generic identifier).
// Ref: Dragon Book §3.3.3 (maximal munch); Wikipedia "Maximal munch".
type LexRuleSet {
  rules: List<LexRule>
}


// -- Token -------------------------------------------------------------------
// A token is the atomic unit emitted by the tokenizer.
// Ref: Wikipedia "Lexical analysis" § Token:
//   "a pair consisting of a token name and an optional attribute value."
//   token name     → class: the terminal symbol / token-category identifier.
//   attribute value → lexeme: the matched character sequence from source.
// Source-span fields (file, start, end) ground in Dragon Book §3.1: tokens carry
// source location for diagnostic reporting and downstream AST construction.
type Token {
  class: Symbol
  lexeme: String
  file: Symbol
  start: Int
  end: Int
}
